## A B S T R A C T
Improving the electrochemiluminescence (ECL) performance of luminophores is an ongoing research hotspot in the ECL realm. Herein, a high-performance metal-organic framework (MOF)-based ECL material $\begin{array}{r l}{{(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(.}}\end{array}$ $\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{HITP}=2,3,6,7,10,11$ 1-hexaiminotriphenylene) with conductivity- and confinement-enhanced ECL was successfully constructed by using conductive MOF $\mathrm{{Ni}}_{3}(\mathrm{{HITP})_{2}}$ as the carrier to graft Ru(bpydc)34- $({\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{bpydc}}=$ $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine- $^{.4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid) into the channels of $\mathrm{{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{{HITP})_{2}}}$ . Compared to $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Ru}}(\mathcal{Q}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Cu}}_{3}(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{HITP}})_{2}$ and $\operatorname{Ru}(\underline{{\omega}}\mathbf{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ with relatively low conductivity, the ECL intensity of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ was prominently increased about 6.76 times and 18.8 times, respectively, which demonstrated that the increase in conductivity induced the ECL enhancement of the MOF-based ECL materials. What’s more, the hydrophobic and porous $\mathrm{{Ni}}_{3}(\mathrm{{HITP})_{2}}$ can not only effectively enrich the lipophilic tripropylamine (TPrA) coreactants in its channels to enhance the electrochemical oxidation efficiency of $\mathrm{{TPrA}}$ , but also provide a conductive reaction microenvironment to boost the ECL reaction between $\mathrm{Ru(bpydc)}_{3}^{3-}$ intermediates and TPrA• in confined spaces, thus realizing a remarkable confinement-enhanced ECL. Considering the excellent ECL performance of $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{{Ru@Ni_{3}(\cdot}}}\end{array}$ $\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , an ultrasensitive ECL biosensor was prepared based on the $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ ECL indicator combining an exonuclease I-aided target cycling amplification strategy for thrombin determination. The constructed ECL biosensor showcased a wide linear range from $1~\mathrm{fM}$ to $1\mathrm{nM}$ with a low detection limit of $_{0.62\mathrm{fM}}$ . Overall, the conductivity- and confinement-enhanced ECL based on $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ provided effective and feasible strategies to enhance ECL performance, which paved a promising avenue for exploring high-efficient MOF-based ECL materials and thus broadened the application scope of conductive MOFs.
## 1. Introduction
Electrochemiluminescence (ECL), as a burgeoning and powerful analytical technology, has aroused considerable interest owing to its integrated advantages of electrochemistry and chemiluminescence (Liu et al., 2015; Hu and Xu, 2010). Currently, the ECL technique has been extensively used in immunoassays and clinical diagnoses attributed to its inherent advantages of near-zero background, simple manipulation, rapid response, high sensitivity, and wide detection range (Babamiri et al., 2019; Muzyka et al., 2017; Qi and Zhang, 2020; Liang et al., 2016;
Kong et al., 2021). As one of the most classic and high-efficient ECL emitters, $\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+}$ and its derivatives possess outstanding ECL per­ formance, good water solubility, excellent redox reversibility, and su­ perior electrochemical properties, and have been widely employed as ECL tags in constructing ultrasensitive ECL sensors (Wang et al., 2018; Yang et al., 2019; Yao et al., 2019). However, the expensive Ru-complexes were severely consumed in liquid phase ECL detection, resulting in high analysis costs and environmental pollution. In contrast to the traditional liquid phase ECL analysis technology, the solid phase ECL technology can fix ECL luminophores on the electrode surface, which not only greatly shortens the distance between the ECL lumino­ phores and the electrode to realize strong ECL emission, but also enables the modified electrode to be reusable, which reduces the waste of expensive ECL reagents and abates pollution simultaneously (Cao et al., 2013). Therefore, it is of great significance to explore applicable carriers to stably immobilize Ru-complexes for constructing solid phase ECL sensors.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as emerging porous materials, have drawn vast interest in the ECL field because their large surface area and highly ordered porosity offer great potential to enhance the loading amount of Ru-complexes (Wang et al., 2021; Fu and Ma, 2020; Hu et al., 2019; Huang et al., 2020; Zhou et al., 2020; Huang et al., 2021; Hu et al., 2018; Yang et al., 2020). However, most MOFs are insulating materials with electrical conductivity $<10^{-10}\mathsf{S c m}^{-1}$ (Meng et al., 2020; Xie et al., 2020), which limits the electrochemical activation of immobilized Ru-complexes, resulting in the low utilization factor of ECL lumino­ phores and the difficulty in further improving the ECL performance. Moreover, due to the low water stability of the majority of MOF mate­ rials, the leaking luminophores in experiments may impact on the sta­ bility of the MOF-based ECL sensors. Besides, the substantial steric encumbrance of Ru-complexes makes it still difficult to load Ru-complexes into the MOF pores which severely restrain the immobi­ lization number of luminophores to a certain degree. Thus, it is very necessary to seek highly conductive and highly stable MOFs with large channels as carrier materials to improve the utilization ratio and loading capacity of Ru-complexes.
Recently, conductive MOFs with outstanding electrical conductivity have attracted worldwide research interest (C.W. Li et al., 2021). With high porosity, well-defined periodic structures, and superior conduc­ tivity, conductive MOFs were ideal for using as active electrode mate­ rials or electronic channels in electrochemical catalysis (Wang et al., 2020), batteries (Lu et al., 2020), and supercapacitors (Wu et al., 2020). More importantly, conductive MOFs can be used as an excellent plat­ form to integrate various functional molecules to design novel hybrid materials with superior electrochemical properties (C. Li et al., 2021). Recently, Dinc˘a’s group has reported a highly stable conductive MOF $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ $\begin{array}{r l r}{\mathrm{(HITP}}&{{}=}&{2,3,6,7,10,11}\end{array}$ -hexaiminotriphenylene) with outstanding conductivity of $55.4~\mathrm{S~cm}^{-1}$ (Chen et al., 2020), which is significantly higher than most MOFs. What’s more, $\mathrm{{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{{HITP})_{2}}}$ has been confirmed to be a positively charged framework with large channels (diameter: ${\sim}1.66\ \mathrm{nm}\ '$ ) (Huang et al., 2021a), therefore it would be possible to graft the negatively charged luminophore $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpydc}})_{3}^{4-}$ (size: ${\sim}1.40\ \mathrm{nm}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bpydc}=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid, Fig. S2) in the channels of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ through electrostatic attraction and coordi­ nation interaction. Thus, we deduced that $\mathrm{{Ni}}_{3}(\mathrm{{HITP})_{2}}$ might be an excellent conductive carrier that could massively and stably immobilize $\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpydc})_{3}^{4-}$ to achieve strong enhancement of ECL emission, which would be highly likely to break a novel path for constructing high-performance MOF-based ECL materials with conductive-enhanced ECL effects.
The other effective strategy for improving the ECL emission is to heighten the production efficiency of coreactant radicals during the ECL reaction. As is well-known, tripropylamine (TPrA) can be used as the coreactant to strongly promote the ECL emission of $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}^{2+}$ de­ rivatives (Lv et al., 2019). However, the electrochemical oxidation ef­ ficiency of TPrA to TPrA radical is severely limited by the low water solubility of TPrA. Considering that the hydrophobic porous $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ (Wu et al., 2017) can efficiently enrich the lipophilic TPrA in its chan­ nels to increase the electrochemical oxidation efficiency of TPrA, it can be expected that the assembly of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Ru(bpydc)}_{3}^{4-}$ will pro­ vide a confined reaction micro-environment to promote the ECL reac­ tion between $\mathrm{Ru(bpydc)}_{3}^{3-}$ intermediates and $\mathrm{{TPrA}^{\bullet}}$ , thereby achieving a remarkable confinement-enhanced ECL. Bearing the above-stated con­ siderations in mind, a novel ECL emitter $\mathrm{Ru(bpydc)_{3}^{4-}}\ @\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP)_{2}}$ (abbreviated as $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2})$ was first synthesized by using the high-stable conductive MOF $\mathrm{{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{{HITP})_{2}}}$ as the carrier to graft Ru (bpydc)34- through electrostatic attraction and coordination interaction between $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ and $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpydc}})_{3}^{4-}$ . Gratifyingly, our experimental results demonstrated that the obtained $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ indeed exhibi­ ted conductivity- and confinement-enhanced ECL effects. In a compar­ ison of ECL signals of $\begin{array}{r}{\operatorname{Ru}\ @\operatorname{Ni}_{3}(\operatorname{HITP})_{2}.}\end{array}$ $\begin{array}{r}{\operatorname{Ru}\ @\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2},}\end{array}$ and $\operatorname{Ru}(\varnothing\operatorname{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2},$ we found that, as expected, the higher the electrical conductivity of MOF carriers $(55.4\ S\ c m^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{{HITP})_{2}}}$ , $0.75\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2},$ and $0.024~\mathrm{S~cm}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2})$ (Chen et al., 2020), the higher the ECL intensity and efficiency of $\begin{array}{r}{\operatorname{Ru}\ @\operatorname{M}_{3}(\operatorname{HITP})_{2}}\end{array}$ $(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Ni}$ , Cu, Co). The ECL intensity and efficiency of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ were about 6.76 times and 4.68 times higher than those of $\operatorname{Ru}(\underline{{\alpha}}\operatorname{Cu}_{3}(\operatorname{HITP})_{2}$ , and 18.8 times and 6.86 times higher than those of $\operatorname{Ru}(\varnothing\operatorname{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ . These results demonstrated that the electrical conductivity enhancement of MOF carriers was beneficial to the improvement of ECL emission, thus providing a hopeful and efficient method for designing and synthesizing high-performance MOF-based ECL emitters. Moreover, this proof-of-concept experiment also proved that $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ could raise the electrochemical oxidation efficiency of TPrA and accelerate the ECL reaction between $\mathrm{Ru(bpydc)}_{3}^{3-}$ intermediates and TPrA• in its confined nanochannels, thus resulting in the prepared $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ with confinement-enhanced ECL effect.
Thrombin (TB) is a serine protease that regulates coagulation, congestion, and inflammation, among other physiological and patho­ logical processes (Jie et al., 2017; Zhang et al., 2020). Therefore, the detection of TB is essential in clinical diagnosis. Given the outstanding ECL performance of $\begin{array}{r}{\operatorname{Ru}(\underline{{\omega}}\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}.}\end{array}$ , here, a new ultrasensitive ECL sensing platform was constructed for TB detection by employing $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ as a high-performance ECL probe together with the exonuclease I (Exo I)-aided target cycling amplification strategy. Initially, as depicted in scheme 1B, $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ was decorated onto the glass carbon electrode (GCE) to attain an extra strong ECL signal. Hereafter, gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) were dripped onto the $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{N}.$ $\mathbf{i}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{GCE}$ to further immobilize the aptamer of TB by Au–S bonds. Afterward, hexanethiol (HT) was dropped on the aptamer/­ $\mathrm{AuNPs/Ru@Ni_{3}(H I T P)_{2}/G C E}$ for blocking the nonspecific sites. Subse­ quently, the ferrocene-labeled single-stranded DNA (Fc-S1) was added and hybridized with the TB aptamer to achieve ECL signal quenching (signal off). After that, under the action of the target TB, Fc-S1 was liberated on account of the hybridization of TB and the TB aptamer. In addition, with the aid of Exo I, the TB aptamer was digested and the target TB was released for recycling. After multiple cycles, numerous Fc-S1 were removed from the electrode interface, resulting in a recov­ ered ECL intensity (signal on). Gratifyingly, the fabricated ECL biosensor realizing the hypersensitive detection of TB. Meaningfully, this work indicated that using conductive MOFs to immobilize ECL luminophores was a hopeful and effective strategy for improving ECL performance, which not only opened new perspectives for exploiting high-efficiency MOF-based ECL emitters with conductivity- and confinement-enhanced $\operatorname{ECL}$ , but also provided new opportunities for fabricating ultrasensitive ECL sensors and expanded the scope of application of conductive MOFs in ECL and biosensing realms.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Synthesis of $N i_{3}(H I T P)_{2}$
The $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ was synthesized in accordance with the literature with some modifications (Chen et al., 2020). Briefly, a solution of 20.85 mg $\left(0.084\ \mathrm{mmol}\right)$ nickel acetate tetrahydrate $(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ in $9\mathrm{mL}$ DMSO was sonicated for $2\mathrm{min}$ and preheated at $65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ The solution was then stirred with a solution of $30~\mathrm{mg}$ (0.056 mmol) HATP⋅6HCl in $9\mathrm{mL}$ water under stirring. After obtaining a homogeneous solution, $12~\mathrm{mL}$ NaOAc aqueous solution $\mathrm{(4~mol~L^{-1}}.$ ) was added, and the mixture was stirred for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ at $65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to obtain a black suspension. Next, the resulting black powder was obtained by centrifugation and washed with ultrapure water and methanol, and then dried in vacuum at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 2.2. Synthesis of $R u@N i_{3}(H I T P)_{2}$
Referring to solvent-assisted ligand incorporation (SALI) procedure (Deria et al., 2013), $20\mathrm{\mg\Ni_{3}(H I T P)_{2}}$ and $13.6\ \mathrm{mg\Ru(H_{2}d c b p y)C l_{2}}$ were added into $20~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF with vigorous stirring at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $36\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then, $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ was obtained by centrifuging and washed with DMF and ultrapure water, and then dried under vacuum at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 2.3. Construction of the ECL sensing platform
Initially, the GCE was pretreated as the previous work (Liang et al., 2016). Then, $12~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ dispersion of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ $\mathrm{(1~\mg/mL)}$ was dropped on the clean GCE to gain a uniform film $(\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{GCE})$ . Afterward, $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of AuNPs dispersion was pipetted onto the $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(-$ $\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{GCE}$ and dried at room temperature to attain the $\mathsf{A u N P s}/\mathsf{R u}@$ ­ $\mathrm{Ni_{3}(H I T P)_{2}/G C E}$ . Subsequently, the prepared aptamer $(10~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was incubated on the $\mathrm{AuNPs/Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{GCE}$ at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. Then, the nonspecific sites were blocked by incubating with $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of hexanethiol (HT, $1.0\ \mathrm{mM}$ , ethanol) for $40\ \mathrm{min}$ to attain aptamer/­ $\mathrm{AuNPs/Ru@Ni_{3}(H I T P)_{2}/G C E}$ . After that, the HT/aptamer/AuNPs/ $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{GCE}$ was incubated with $10\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of Fc-S1 $(2\upmu\mathrm{M})$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . Then, the Fc-S1/HT/aptamer $\mathrm{\langleAuNPs/Ru@Ni_{3}(H I T P)_{2}/G C E}$ was incu­ bated with $10\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of a mixture including various concentrations of TB and $10\mathrm{U}$ Exo I at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2.5\mathrm{h}$ . After every modified step, it is necessary to rinse each resulting electrode gently with ultrapure water to remove any excess reagents.
## 3. Results and discussion
3.1. Characterization of $N i_{3}(H I T P)_{2}$ and $R u@N i_{3}(H I T P)_{2}$
The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ exhibited a series of prominent peaks at $2\theta=4.7^{\circ}$ , $9.5^{\circ}$ , $12.6^{\circ}$ , $16.5^{\circ}$ , and $27.3^{\circ}$ , which matched well with the simulated PXRD pattern of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ (Fig. 1A) and thus demonstrated that the crys­ tallinity and framework structure of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ were well preserved. In addition, the size and morphology of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ (Fig. 1B) and $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ (Fig. 1C and Fig. S3) were investigated by scanning electron microscopy (SEM). The $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ both consisted of stacking nanoparticles with the size of $100{-}200~\mathrm{nm}$ . Addi­ tionally, the energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) mapping images of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{N}.$ $\mathrm{i}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ (Fig. S4) proved that Ni and Ru elements were uniformly distributed in this framework. Moreover, compared with [Ru $\mathrm{(H_{2}b p y d c)_{3}]C l_{2}}$ , the peak at $1717~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ attributable to the protonated carboxyl group disappeared in the Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectrum of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ (Fig. S5), demonstrating the coordination between the carboxyl groups of $\mathrm{Ru(bpydc)}_{3}^{4-}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ . In the UV–vis absorption spectroscopy of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ (Fig. S6), the peaks at 302 nm and 467 nm could be attributed to $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpydc}})_{3}^{4\cdot}$ , confirming that Ru (bpydc)34- was grafted into $\mathrm{{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{{HITP})_{2}}}$ . To prove the electrostatic attraction between $\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpydc})_{3}^{4-}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , $\zeta$ -potential character­ ization was carried out (Figs. S7A and S7B). After immobilizing Ru (bpydc $)_{3}^{4-}$ into $\mathrm{{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{{HITP})_{2}}}$ , the $\zeta$ -potential was negatively shifted from $20.5~\mathrm{mV}$ to $-21.1\ \mathrm{mV}_{\mathrm{~i~}}$ , since the positively charged $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ was neutralized by negatively charged $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpydc}})_{3}^{4-}$ . As shown in Fig. S7C, after the grafting of $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpydc}})_{3}^{4-}$ , $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ possessed an average diameter of around $955\mathrm{nm}$ , which was larger than the average diameter of $\mathrm{{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{{HITP})_{2}}}$ $(255\ \mathrm{nm})$ . Furthermore, the molar ratio of $\mathrm{{Ru/Ni}}$ in $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ was indicated to be 0.265:1 by the inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES) (the details were shown in the Supporting Information, Section S10). The above results demonstrated the triumphant synthesis of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ .
## 3.2. Luminescence properties of the $R u@N i_{3}(H I T P)_{2}$
The luminescence properties of the $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ were explored by measuring photoluminescence (PL) and ECL spectra. As illustrated in Fig. 2A, the ECL-wavelength-potential 3D surface image clearly dis­ played the evolution process of ECL emission of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ . Meanwhile, as exhibited in Fig. 2B and C, it could be observed that the maximal ECL emission peak at $638\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Furthermore, an emission maximum at $613~\mathrm{{nm}}$ was observed in the PL spectrum of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(-$ $\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ (Fig. 2C). The maximum ECL emission disclosed a slight $25\mathrm{-nm}$ red-shift contrast to the PL emission peak, presumably due to instru­ mental effects or self-absorption (Rashidnadimi et al., 2008; Swanick et al., 2015).
3.3. Conductivity- and confinement-enhanced ECL investigation of $R u@N i_{3}(H I T P)_{2}$
To demonstrate the validity of the conductivity-enhanced ECL effect of $\begin{array}{r}{\operatorname{Ru}\ @\operatorname{Ni}_{3}(\operatorname{HITP})_{2}.}\end{array}$ , the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) Nyquist plots of various modified electrodes were measured and the corresponding electron transfer resistance $\mathrm{(R_{et})}$ values were calculated based on the equivalent circuit (Fig. 3A, inset) with model impedance data. As shown in Fig. 3A, when the HATP was decorated on the GCE, a relatively high $\mathrm{R_{et}}$ of $564.4\Omega$ was obtained because HATP could block the electron transfer. However, when $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ was modified on the GCE, the $\mathrm{R_{et}}$ value $\left(136.8\ \Omega\right)$ decreased visibly thanks to the exceptional conductivity of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ . Meanwhile, $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(-$ $\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{GCE}$ exhibited a considerably lower $\mathrm{R_{et}}$ value than the $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{GCE}$ $346.1\ \Omega)$ and $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}/\mathrm{GCE}$ $(423.9\ \Omega)$ , which demonstrated that $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ possessed higher electrical conductivity. Furthermore, to confirm that the enhancement of con­ ductivity could facilitate the improvement of ECL emission of MOFbased ECL materials, we compared the ECL intensity and efficiency of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , and $\operatorname{Ru}(\underline{{\omega}}\mathbf{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ including the same amount of $\mathrm{Ru(bpydc)}_{3}^{4-}$ under identical conditions (Fig. 3B and Table S2). It was found that, as expected, the higher the electrical con­ ductivity of MOF carriers $(55.4\ S\ c m^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{{HITP})_{2}}}$ , $0.75\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{{HITP})_{2}}}$ and $0.024~\mathrm{S~cm}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2})$ (Chen et al., 2020), the higher the ECL signal and efficiency of $\begin{array}{r}{\operatorname{Ru}\ @\operatorname{M}_{3}(\operatorname{HITP})_{2}}\end{array}$ $(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Ni}$ , Cu, Co). The ECL intensity and efficiency of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ were about 6.76 times and 4.68 times higher than those of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ , and 18.8 times and 6.86 times higher than those of $\operatorname{Ru}(\underline{{\omega}}\mathbf{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ . This proof-of-concept experiment validated that conductivity improvement was conducive to the ECL enhancement of the MOF-based ECL materials. In addition, ECL efficiency of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ also showed a significant advantage compared with other reported materials (Table S2), which indicated that using highly conductive MOFs as the carriers to fix ECL luminophores was an admirable method to improve the ECL efficiency. To validate the correctness of the confinement-enhanced ECL effect, a comparison experiment of ECL-potential profiles between bare GCE and $\mathrm{Ni_{3}(H I T P)_{2}/G C E}$ in $\mathrm{Ru(bpydc)_{3}^{4-}+T P r A}$ solution (Fig. 3C) was carried out under the same experimental conditions, which showed that the ECL signal of $\mathrm{Ni_{3}(H I T P)_{2}/G C E}$ has increased about 4.14-fold compared with that of bare GCE, demonstrating the notably confinement-enhanced ECL effect. Furthermore, cyclic voltammogram (CV) scans on GCE and the $\mathrm{Ni_{3}(H I T P)_{2}/G C E}$ were performed in TPrA solution to investigate the possible reasons for the confinementenhanced ECL effect of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ (Fig. 3D). An apparent anodic peak at $0.87{\mathrm{V}}$ could be seen for the bare GCE (Fig. 3D, curve b), which was due to the electrochemical oxidation of TPrA. Whereas, the oxida­ tion peak current was increased nearly 1.3-fold after the modification of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ onto GCE (Fig. 3D, curve a), indicating that $\mathrm{{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{{HITP})_{2}}}$ indeed raised the electrochemical oxidation rate of TPrA. According to the above experimental results, we speculated that the confinementenhanced ECL effect of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ might be ascribed to the following reasons. Firstly, the hydrophobic channels of $\mathrm{{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{{HITP})_{2}}}$ could effectively enrich the lipophilic TPrA to raise the electrochemical oxidation rate of TPrA, leading to the production of more TPrA•. Sec­ ondly, the porous $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ provided a confined reaction microenvironment to accelerate the ECL reaction between $\mathrm{Ru(bpydc)}_{3}^{3-}$ in­ termediates and TPrA•, thus achieving a conspicuously enhanced ECL emission.
