## 3.5. Detection of target TB with the designed biosensor
To study the sensitivity of the prepared ECL sensor for detecting TB, the ECL signals of the constructed biosensor that was incubated with a series of concentrations of target TB were monitored (Fig. 4) under optimum conditions (Fig. S11). We found that the ECL signals were sequentially enhanced with increasing TB concentration in the range from 1 fM to $1\mathrm{nM}$ (Fig. 4A). Meanwhile, as revealed in Fig. 4B, the ECL intensity $(I)$ linearly responded to the logarithm of TB concentrations (c) with the linear equation $I=8833.3+2495.1\mathrm{lg}[c/(\mathrm{pM})]$ $(\mathbf{R}=0.9996)$ . Moreover, we calculated that the detection limit was 0.62 fM $(\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{N}=3)$ ) (Nie et al., 2019; Long, 1983). Satisfactorily, compared with some pre­ vious detection methods for TB (Table 1), this biosensor exhibited higher sensitivity and a wider detection range.
## 3.6. Selectivity, reproducibility and stability of the ECL biosensor
Selectivity, reproducibility, and stability are three vital factors to estimate the performance of an ECL biosensor. As illustrated in Fig. 5A, the selectivity of the biosensor was investigated via incubating with 100 fM of different interferences such as uric acid (UA), β2-microglobulin (β2-MG), $\upalpha$ -1-fetoprotein (AFP), cholesterol and mucin 1 (MUC1). It could be observed that there were negligible changes in ECL responses between these interferences (each of $100\ \mathrm{fM})$ and the blank sample. However, when the proposed biosensor was incubated with the mixtures containing MUC1 (100 fM), b2-MG (100 fM), AFP (100 fM), cholesterol (100 fM), UA $(100~\mathrm{fM})$ , and TB (10 fM), the ECL response increased visibly and had no significant difference with that of pure TB (10 fM). These results demonstrated the wonderful selectivity of the prepared ECL biosensor for TB assay. As shown in Fig. 5B, we estimated the reproducibility of the developed biosensor with five parallel tests of 1 pM TB. The calculated relative standard deviation (RSD) value was $0.93\%$ , indicating the outstanding reproducibility of the ECL biosensor. To rate the stability of the constructed biosensor, the ECL responses were detected at $100\mathrm{pM}$ TB (Fig. 5C) and 1 fM TB (Fig. 5D). As a result, the ECL intensity had scarcely any variation under continuous cyclic scan­ ning for 20 cycles and the RSD were calculated to be $0.47\%$ and $1.77\%$ , respectively, which indicated that the biosensor possessed outstanding stability.
## 3.7. Application of the ECL biosensor in human serum samples
To assess the practicability of the fabricated ECL biosensor, recovery assays were adopted by measuring TB in human serum samples. As a result, the recoveries of TB were $99.7\%$ , $103.0\%$ , $104.1\%$ , $102.8\%$ , and the corresponding RSD $(\mathfrak{n}=3)$ were $2.50\%$ , $2.64\%$ , $4.86\%$ , $3.23\%$ (Table 2) suggesting that the ECL biosensor had a favorable application prospect for detecting TB in practical samples.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, by the assembly of $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpydc}})_{3}^{4-}$ within conductive MOF $\mathrm{{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{{HITP})_{2}}}$ via electrostatic attraction and coordination interaction, a high-efficient ECL emitter $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ with conductivity- and confinement-enhanced ECL effects was successfully prepared, which realized a conspicuous ECL enhancement compared with lowconductive $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ and $\operatorname{Ru}(\underline{{\alpha}}\operatorname{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ . The excellent ECL performance of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ not only stemmed from the highconductive $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ that accelerated charge transport to promote the electrochemical excitation of $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathrm{bpydc}})_{3}^{4-}$ , but also originated from the confined reaction micro-environment of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ that favored the electrochemical oxidation of TPrA and boosted the ECL reaction be­ tween $\mathrm{Ru(bpydc)}_{3}^{3-}$ intermediates and $\mathrm{\DeltaTPrA^{\bullet}}$ . Based on the outstanding ECL property of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ and Exo I-assisted target cycling amplification strategy, a novel ECL sensor was fabricated and achieved supersensitive TB detection. In short, this proof-of-concept study pro­ vided promising and efficient strategies to boost ECL performance, thereby opening a new horizon to devise and construct high-efficient ECL materials and expanding the application field of conductive MOFs.
